00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.077
Quiz time: Are the following words
Chinese or Japanese?

00:00:03.077 --> 00:00:03.577
First.

00:00:04.997 --> 00:00:06.277
Yes it's Japanese.

00:00:06.457 --> 00:00:06.957
Next.

00:00:08.197 --> 00:00:09.237
It's Chinese.

00:00:09.657 --> 00:00:10.157
Next.

00:00:11.377 --> 00:00:13.597
Actually, this is both Chinese and Japanese.

00:00:13.597 --> 00:00:14.517
And finally.

00:00:15.417 --> 00:00:17.777
Actually this is neither, this is Thai.

00:00:18.187 --> 00:00:21.107
What, you thought all Asian languages are the same? Racist.

00:00:25.537 --> 00:00:28.717
Hello everyone. Welcome to the LangFocus channel, 
my name is Paul.

00:00:28.717 --> 00:00:33.117
Today's topic is: the question how similar are Chinese and Japanese.

00:00:33.637 --> 00:00:38.237
A lot of people look at a map of Asia and see how close Japan and China are or they might look

00:00:38.237 --> 00:00:41.397
at a sample of Chinese and Japanese
writing and think that something looks

00:00:41.397 --> 00:00:45.296
similar and they might conclude that the
two languages are similar, but are they

00:00:45.296 --> 00:00:49.077
really similar? Well, the short answer is
no they are completely different

00:00:49.077 --> 00:00:53.007
languages but Japanese has been highly
influenced by Chinese in its vocabulary

00:00:53.007 --> 00:00:54.697
and its writing system

00:00:54.697 --> 00:00:58.347
Chinese and Japanese do not share any
common origin and they don't belong to

00:00:58.347 --> 00:00:59.907
the same language family

00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:03.457
Chinese languages belong to the
Sino-Tibetan language family and

00:01:03.457 --> 00:01:06.817
Japanese as far as we know belongs
 to the Japonic language family.

00:01:06.817 --> 00:01:10.277
Phonology: First off, the phonology of 
the languages is very different

00:01:10.277 --> 00:01:14.857
With the most basic difference being that Chinese is a tonal language while Japanese is not.

00:01:14.857 --> 00:01:19.157
A tonal language is one in which there are tones or pitches that help determine the meaning

00:01:19.157 --> 00:01:23.417
of the words you use; if you used the
wrong tone then the meaning of the word changes.

00:01:23.417 --> 00:01:26.997
Here's an example: the word for teacher, "lao3shi1"

00:01:26.997 --> 00:01:30.397
and the word for naive, "lao3shi2"

00:01:30.397 --> 00:01:33.757
and the word for old-fashioned, "lao3shi4".

00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:37.977
So I'm sure that Chinese teachers get
called naive and old-fashioned all the time by mistake

00:01:37.977 --> 00:01:39.777
or maybe not by mistake.

00:01:39.777 --> 00:01:43.977
In Japanese there are basically no tones like this that determine the meaning of words.

00:01:43.977 --> 00:01:48.137
In Japanese there is something called pitch accent which means that the syllables of a word have

00:01:48.137 --> 00:01:51.517
either a higher pitch or a lower pitch
but they're not like the tones in

00:01:51.517 --> 00:01:54.646
Chinese because they typically don't
determine the meaning of the word

00:01:54.646 --> 00:01:56.326
they're more like stress in English.

00:01:57.317 --> 00:02:01.377
Structure: the structure and grammar of the two languages is completely different

00:02:01.377 --> 00:02:06.897
at the most basic level Chinese is an
SVO language while Japanese is an SOV language

00:02:06.897 --> 00:02:12.997
Here's an example in Chinese: "ta1shuo1zhong1wen2" This means he speaks Chinese and you can see

00:02:12.997 --> 00:02:16.577
SVO: subject verb object.

00:02:16.577 --> 00:02:19.157
And here's that sentence translated into Japanese:

00:02:19.157 --> 00:02:21.197
"kare wa chūgokugo wo hanashimasu".

00:02:21.677 --> 00:02:25.137
Here we can see SOV: subject object verb.

00:02:25.137 --> 00:02:29.377
And you can see there are some extra little words in Japanese that don't exist in the Chinese sentence

00:02:29.377 --> 00:02:33.377
for example Japanese has the
topic marker "wa" which doesn't exist in Chinese

00:02:33.377 --> 00:02:37.677
and Japanese also has the
object marker "wo" which doesn't exist in Chinese.

00:02:38.877 --> 00:02:42.297
Chinese is an analytic language
which basically means that you create a

00:02:42.297 --> 00:02:47.097
sentence by placing independent elements
side-by-side. Analytic languages like

00:02:47.097 --> 00:02:51.526
Chinese do not use inflection: inflection
means modifying words to indicate some

00:02:51.526 --> 00:02:55.166
kind of grammatical meaning like plural,
gender, tense or case

00:02:55.166 --> 00:02:58.357
You don't change any part of a 
word in order to change its meaning

00:02:58.366 --> 00:03:02.776
Instead you can add to the meaning of that word by adding an extra word next to it

00:03:02.776 --> 00:03:06.136
Japanese on the other hand is an agglutinative language that uses inflection.

00:03:06.137 --> 00:03:08.597
Agglutinative languages use a lot of affixes:

00:03:08.597 --> 00:03:13.537
Rather than just placing independent words side-by-side
you attach additional pieces to words to

00:03:13.547 --> 00:03:17.337
add or modify meaning: this kind of
inflection in Japanese means that

00:03:17.337 --> 00:03:20.897
Japanese words are often longer than
Chinese words and that it may take a few

00:03:20.897 --> 00:03:23.797
words in Chinese to translate one Japanese word.

00:03:24.137 --> 00:03:27.077
In these Chinese sentences we 
can see that there's no inflection

00:03:27.077 --> 00:03:29.697
"wo3qu4xue2xiao4"

00:03:29.697 --> 00:03:31.897
this sentence means I go to school

00:03:31.897 --> 00:03:34.097
"wo3qu4xue2xiao4"

00:03:34.097 --> 00:03:36.297
this sentence means I went  to school

00:03:36.297 --> 00:03:39.257
so no part of the verb is changed indicate the past tense

00:03:39.257 --> 00:03:42.097
We can show the past tense by
just adding a time expression.

00:03:42.097 --> 00:03:44.897
Now these similar sentences in
Japanese show inflection

00:03:44.897 --> 00:03:48.237
This sentence means I go to school 
"watashi wa gakkou e iku"

00:03:48.237 --> 00:03:50.937
I went to school 
"watashi wa gakkou e itta"

00:03:50.937 --> 00:03:54.717
And the polite form of "I go to school":
"watashi wa gakkou e ikimasu"

00:03:55.357 --> 00:03:58.637
I want to go to school:
"watashi wa gakkou e ikitai".

00:03:58.637 --> 00:04:03.417
In this Japanese example we use inflection
changing the word "iku" to the "iki" form

00:04:03.417 --> 00:04:07.417
and then we use agglutination to attach
a form that shows intention: "tai".

00:04:07.997 --> 00:04:12.297
In Chinese though there's no inflection or
agglutination: you just add an extra word like this.

00:04:12.957 --> 00:04:17.117
That's just a brief sample of how different 
the grammar is in Japanese and Chinese

00:04:17.117 --> 00:04:20.717
basically they're completely different languages when it comes to their grammatical structure

00:04:22.857 --> 00:04:26.997
The one area which there is some similarity between
Chinese and Japanese is in vocabulary

00:04:27.007 --> 00:04:30.547
and in the use of Chinese characters.
During a period of great Chinese

00:04:30.547 --> 00:04:34.107
influence between the 5th and 9th centuries CE
 there was a huge amount

00:04:34.107 --> 00:04:37.027
of borrowing from early Middle Chinese
into Japanese.

00:04:37.027 --> 00:04:41.347
Japanese originally had no written form
so Chinese began to be used as the literary language

00:04:41.347 --> 00:04:43.927
and as the language of science and of religion.

00:04:43.927 --> 00:04:46.317
Chinese vocabulary began to be borrowed into

00:04:46.317 --> 00:04:49.967
Japanese and the Chinese writing system
was gradually adapted to fit the

00:04:49.967 --> 00:04:52.767
Japanese language so not only
were vocabulary borrowed

00:04:52.767 --> 00:04:55.887
But the Chinese characters that represent them were also adopted.

00:04:55.887 --> 00:05:01.287
60% of the words used in Japanese are of Chinese origin but that includes all of the

00:05:01.297 --> 00:05:04.547
words in the dictionary and that
includes a lot of very specialized, academic

00:05:04.547 --> 00:05:07.397
and formal vocabulary that's mainly used in writing.

00:05:07.407 --> 00:05:11.027
In spoken Japanese the number of Chinese loan words used as much lower,

00:05:11.027 --> 00:05:13.137
At about 18%. The borrowed words

00:05:13.137 --> 00:05:17.917
were almost all Chinese nouns; even though in Japanese they might be used as verbs or as adjectives

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:20.877
that's just one way in which the
vocabulary is used differently

00:05:20.887 --> 00:05:24.376
but also the pronunciation of those
Chinese words that were borrowed into

00:05:24.376 --> 00:05:28.117
Japanese changed to match the Japanese 
phonological system and that included

00:05:28.117 --> 00:05:32.677
the loss of the tones of those words.
And also these words were borrowed a long time ago,

00:05:32.677 --> 00:05:35.857
which means that the words have
also changed in pronunciation in Chinese

00:05:35.857 --> 00:05:38.197
so the pronunciation has diverged quite a bit,

00:05:38.197 --> 00:05:40.477
meaning that modern Japanese pronunciation and

00:05:40.477 --> 00:05:44.697
modern Chinese pronunciation of similar characters or words is quite different.

00:05:44.697 --> 00:05:49.417
And it's also important to point out that Mandarin was not the standard form of Chinese back in those days

00:05:49.417 --> 00:05:52.397
when those vocabulary words were
borrowed into Japanese, so

00:05:52.397 --> 00:05:56.857
Even at the time they were borrowed the pronunciation was different from standard Chinese today

00:05:56.857 --> 00:06:00.177
And from what I understand the modern Japanese pronunciation of those loanwords

00:06:00.177 --> 00:06:03.477
Is often more similar to modern Cantonese
than it is to Mandarin

00:06:03.977 --> 00:06:08.577
These examples show how the same words are pronounced very differently in Japanese and Mandarin

00:06:08.577 --> 00:06:11.477
The word for family: In Japanese it's "katei"

00:06:11.477 --> 00:06:14.757
and actually in Japanese the meaning is more like household or home

00:06:14.757 --> 00:06:17.077
And in Mandarin: "jia1ting2".

00:06:17.397 --> 00:06:20.277
Next, the word for death:
In Japanese: "shibou"

00:06:20.297 --> 00:06:22.217
And in Mandarin: "si3wang2"

00:06:22.677 --> 00:06:25.357
Next, the word for season:
In Japanese, "kisetsu"

00:06:25.357 --> 00:06:27.797
And in Mandarin: "ji4jie2"

00:06:27.797 --> 00:06:30.817
And the word for home country:
"In Japanese: bokoku"

00:06:30.817 --> 00:06:32.877
And in Mandarin: "mu3guo2"

00:06:33.337 --> 00:06:36.237
So you can see that these words
look the same and they have basically

00:06:36.237 --> 00:06:38.277
the same meaning but they sound very different

00:06:38.277 --> 00:06:43.157
So Chinese vocabulary represented by Chinese characters were borrowed into Japanese

00:06:43.157 --> 00:06:47.297
but those Chinese characters were also then applied to native Japanese vocabulary

00:06:47.297 --> 00:06:49.837
that had a related meaning to those Chinese loan words

00:06:49.837 --> 00:06:53.417
That means that a Chinese speaker can often look at a native Japanese word and

00:06:53.417 --> 00:06:56.497
understand its core meaning without knowing that Japanese word because

00:06:56.497 --> 00:06:58.917
the Chinese character is used to represent it

00:06:58.917 --> 00:07:02.457
So Chinese people can often read a
text in Japanese and they can make sense

00:07:02.466 --> 00:07:07.246
of the basic meaning of it based on the Chinese characters but they won't understand all the details

00:07:07.246 --> 00:07:10.646
And the same is true in reverse: Japanese people can look at a Chinese text

00:07:10.646 --> 00:07:12.446
and kind of make sense of the meaning of it

00:07:12.446 --> 00:07:14.597
based on the Chinese characters that they know.

00:07:14.597 --> 00:07:17.297
But it is easy to misunderstand the
details of what's written

00:07:17.297 --> 00:07:20.217
especially if they have zero knowledge
of the other language

00:07:20.217 --> 00:07:23.797
Here are some Japanese example sentences that have the same Chinese characters

00:07:23.797 --> 00:07:26.697
but the meaning is very different because of the inflection that's used

00:07:26.697 --> 00:07:30.037
"Neko wa sakana wo taberu". That means,
The cat eats fish.

00:07:30.037 --> 00:07:34.137
"Neko wa sakana wo tabenai". That means,
The cat doesn't eat fish.

00:07:34.137 --> 00:07:38.676
"Neko wa sakana wo tabeta". That means,
The cat ate fish, in the past tense.

00:07:38.676 --> 00:07:42.256
"Neko wa sakana wo taberutsumori"
the cat plans to eat fish

00:07:42.256 --> 00:07:46.696
"Neko wa sakana ni teberareta", that means
The cat was eaten by a fish

00:07:46.697 --> 00:07:48.717
So a Chinese character reading these sentences would

00:07:48.717 --> 00:07:51.177
get the core meaning of the Chinese characters

00:07:51.177 --> 00:07:53.357
But they would miss something in the inflection

00:07:53.357 --> 00:07:57.697
For example the negative, or the past tense,
or intention or the passive form

00:07:57.707 --> 00:08:00.707
especially the last example could be
highly misunderstood

00:08:00.707 --> 00:08:03.556
So imagine you're an English speaker
reading something with English loanwords

00:08:03.557 --> 00:08:05.137
it might look something like this

00:08:05.137 --> 00:08:07.117
Cat bla fish blah food blah blah

00:08:07.117 --> 00:08:11.057
Of course even after just a little bit of studying Japanese a Chinese speaker could probably

00:08:11.057 --> 00:08:14.517
learn enough to understand basic
Japanese sentences like that

00:08:14.517 --> 00:08:19.157
For me, as someone who studied Japanese to a relatively advanced level the same is true in reverse:

00:08:19.157 --> 00:08:23.997
when I was in Taiwan last year on the subway for example I could read a lot of the advertisements

00:08:23.997 --> 00:08:28.097
and I would get most of the meaning; I wouldn't understand all of the Chinese characters

00:08:28.097 --> 00:08:32.377
but I would get usually 3 out of 4 of them
and the fourth one would be new to me

00:08:32.377 --> 00:08:36.757
because in Chinese there are a lot of characters that actually aren't used in Japanese at all

00:08:36.757 --> 00:08:40.417
But just getting 3 out of 4 of them was often
enough to understand the basic meaning

00:08:40.427 --> 00:08:43.877
of that advertising but of course I had
no idea how to pronounce those Chinese

00:08:43.877 --> 00:08:47.167
characters and if I tried to read them
out loud like the Japanese I would hear

00:08:47.167 --> 00:08:50.347
laughter from both my girlfriend and
from local bystanders.

00:08:50.347 --> 00:08:54.447
It might seem strange that I can look at a Chinese
character in Chinese and understand what

00:08:54.447 --> 00:08:58.397
it means without knowing how to say it
but think of it as a symbol like a number

00:08:58.397 --> 00:09:01.597
the number seven right here is
pronounced differently in different languages

00:09:01.597 --> 00:09:04.977
but when you see it you know
what it means no matter how you pronounce it

00:09:04.977 --> 00:09:09.417
Chinese characters are kind of like that except that they represent a much wider range of meanings

00:09:09.417 --> 00:09:12.237
The Chinese characters
used in any particular sentence can be

00:09:12.237 --> 00:09:16.317
very different from those used in the
other language for a sentence of similar meaning

00:09:16.317 --> 00:09:19.357
and in Japanese there are a lot
of compound words that were created from

00:09:19.357 --> 00:09:23.357
Chinese characters but those compound
words don't actually exist in Chinese

00:09:23.357 --> 00:09:28.217
these are called "wasei kango" which means
something like Chinese vocabulary created in Japan

00:09:28.557 --> 00:09:32.357
Here are some examples of "wasei kango": some were created to represent things

00:09:32.357 --> 00:09:35.477
unique to Japan like "ninja" "geisha"

00:09:35.477 --> 00:09:39.157
and others were created during the Meiji period to represent Western concepts

00:09:39.157 --> 00:09:41.377
like democracy, "minshu".

00:09:42.317 --> 00:09:45.297
So even though you might recognize
a lot of characters when you look at

00:09:45.297 --> 00:09:48.457
some text in the other language they
might be used in a very different way

00:09:48.457 --> 00:09:49.937
so the meeting will be unclear

00:09:49.937 --> 00:09:54.197
A Chinese speaker might be confused by "wasei kango" when they try to read some Japanese

00:09:54.197 --> 00:09:57.537
and Japanese people might be confused
when they read Chinese because

00:09:57.537 --> 00:10:01.617
there are a lot of Chinese characters that were never borrowed into Japanese in the first place

00:10:01.617 --> 00:10:03.677
or they're not used in modern Japanese

00:10:03.677 --> 00:10:06.817
But in either case, even basic knowledge
of the other language

00:10:06.817 --> 00:10:09.217
would help in reading comprehension quite a bit

00:10:09.217 --> 00:10:13.137
Japanese "returned loan words" in
Chinese so as we've discussed already

00:10:13.137 --> 00:10:16.397
lots of Chinese vocabulary was
borrowed into Japanese

00:10:16.397 --> 00:10:19.937
but some Japanese vocabulary has also
been borrowed into Chinese.

00:10:19.937 --> 00:10:24.357
A moment ago I mentioned "wasei kango"
Japanese vocabulary created from Chinese roots

00:10:24.357 --> 00:10:28.457
vocabulary that didn't exist in Chinese
but some of those "wasei kango"

00:10:28.467 --> 00:10:31.487
have actually been borrowed from
Japanese into Chinese

00:10:31.487 --> 00:10:36.767
Some sources say that such vocabulary accounts for around 30% of modern Chinese vocabulary.

00:10:37.417 --> 00:10:39.897
Of course the way those returned loan words are pronounced

00:10:39.897 --> 00:10:43.337
is based on the modern Chinese pronunciation of the Chinese characters

00:10:43.837 --> 00:10:44.997
Here are some examples:

00:10:44.997 --> 00:10:49.157
The word for history in Japanese, "rekishi" and
Mandarin, "li4shi3"

00:10:49.157 --> 00:10:54.377
The word for industry, in Japanese, "kōgyō"
In Mandarin, "gong1ye4"

00:10:54.377 --> 00:11:00.157
The word for electron or electronic in Japanese "denshi"
In Mandarin "dian4zi3"

00:11:01.497 --> 00:11:05.977
The word for injection, in Japanese "chūsha"
In Mandarin "zhu4she4"

00:11:05.977 --> 00:11:11.177
The word for philosophy in Japanese "tetsugaku"
In Mandarin "zhe2xue2"

00:11:11.177 --> 00:11:16.397
The word for system in Japanese "keitō"
And in Mandarin "xi4tong2"

00:11:16.937 --> 00:11:19.497
And most Chinese people these days
are probably not even aware

00:11:19.497 --> 00:11:21.737
that those words were borrowed from Japanese

00:11:21.737 --> 00:11:24.417
It's also important to point out that in mainland China,

00:11:24.417 --> 00:11:28.737
Simplified Chinese characters are now used as
opposed to the traditional Chinese characters

00:11:28.737 --> 00:11:30.477
used in Taiwan and in Hong Kong

00:11:30.477 --> 00:11:34.677
That means that someone from mainland China may have some more trouble recognizing Japanese kanji

00:11:34.677 --> 00:11:36.717
than someone from Taiwan for example

00:11:36.717 --> 00:11:39.837
So in short, Chinese and Japanese are
very different languages

00:11:39.837 --> 00:11:43.497
especially the spoken languages
even though there was a lot of vocabulary

00:11:43.497 --> 00:11:45.377
borrowed from Chinese into Japanese

00:11:45.377 --> 00:11:47.777
And a little bit from Japanese into Chinese

00:11:47.777 --> 00:11:50.957
Only in writing are those two languages
somewhat intelligible

00:11:50.957 --> 00:11:53.337
because of the Chinese characters that are used

00:11:53.337 --> 00:11:55.477
So the question of the day for Japanese speakers:

00:11:55.477 --> 00:11:57.977
"What's your experience when you
look at a Chinese text?"

00:11:57.977 --> 00:12:01.857
"Are you able to understand the basic meaning
based on the Chinese characters?"

00:12:01.857 --> 00:12:05.817
And similarly for Chinese speakers: "What happens when you look at a Japanese text?"

00:12:05.817 --> 00:12:11.337
"Can you understand the basic meaning or did you get confused because of the different way that the Chinese characters are used?"

00:12:11.337 --> 00:12:14.577
Thanks again to all my Pateron supporters and I
want to say thanks to all the

00:12:14.577 --> 00:12:17.457
New subscribers who joined this month;
there are a lot of you

00:12:17.457 --> 00:12:19.917
And you are very welcome and very appreciated

00:12:19.917 --> 00:12:22.317
Thank you for watching, have a nice day

